  realLife: [
    { tag: 'CORRECTION', tagZh: '受教', verseRef: 'Proverbs 29:1',
      titleZh: '当同一句提醒又出现', titleEn: 'When the same warning comes again',
      bodyZh: '“人屡次受责罚，仍然硬着颈项，他必顷刻败坏，无法可治”（29:1）。有些提醒会反复出现——同一个朋友、同一位家人、甚至内心同一个声音，一次次指向同一件事。我们很容易把它当作唠叨挡回去。但这节经文提醒：反复无视，会让心一点点变硬，直到某天崩塌得措手不及。今天不妨认真想一想：最近反复出现在我耳边的，是哪一句话？它会不会正是恩典？',
      bodyEn: 'He who is often rebuked and stiffens his neck will be destroyed suddenly, with no remedy (29:1). Some warnings keep recurring — the same friend, the same family member, even the same inner voice, pointing again and again at the same thing. It’s easy to deflect it as nagging. But this verse warns that repeatedly ignoring it hardens the heart bit by bit, until one day the collapse comes without warning. Ask honestly today: what is the message that keeps returning to me? Could it actually be grace?' },
    { tag: 'ANGER & CONFLICT', tagZh: '愤怒与冲突', verseRef: 'Proverbs 29:11',
      titleZh: '把情绪“发出来”一定好吗？', titleEn: 'Is “letting it out” always good?',
      bodyZh: '“愚妄人怒气全发；智慧人忍气含怒”（29:11）。我们的文化常说，压抑情绪不健康，要勇敢表达。这有一定道理，但箴言提醒另一面：把怒气不加节制地一股脑倒出来，往往伤人伤己，事后追悔。忍住怒气不等于假装没事，而是先给自己一点空间——深呼吸、暂停、把话想清楚，再决定如何回应。这不是软弱，而是被圣灵管理的力量。',
      bodyEn: 'A fool vents all of his anger, but a wise man brings himself under control (29:11). Our culture often says bottling emotions is unhealthy and we should express ourselves boldly. There’s truth there, but Proverbs names the other side: dumping anger out unchecked usually wounds others and ourselves, and we regret it later. Restraining anger isn’t pretending nothing’s wrong; it’s giving yourself a little space first — a breath, a pause, thinking the words through — before deciding how to respond. That’s not weakness; it’s strength under the Spirit’s governance.' },
    { tag: 'PARENTING', tagZh: '教养孩子', verseRef: 'Proverbs 29:17',
      titleZh: '管教是爱，不是控制', titleEn: 'Discipline is love, not control',
      bodyZh: '“管教你的儿子，他就使你得安息，也必使你心里喜乐”（29:17）。放任孩子任意而行，短期省事，长期却让人心痛（29:15）。这里说的管教，不是发泄怒气、也不是苛待，而是肯花心力去引导、立界限、教导是非——正因为爱，所以不省这份力气。原则是普世的：真正为一个人好，有时意味着说“不”，并陪他一起承担成长的过程。',
      bodyEn: 'Correct your son, and he will give you peace; yes, he will bring delight to your soul (29:17). Leaving a child to do as he pleases is easier now but painful later (29:15). The discipline here is not venting anger or being harsh, but the willingness to spend effort guiding, setting boundaries, teaching right from wrong — precisely because you love, you don’t spare that effort. The principle is universal: truly wanting someone’s good sometimes means saying “no,” and walking with them through the process of growth.' },
    { tag: 'FEAR OF MAN', tagZh: '怕人', verseRef: 'Proverbs 29:25',
      titleZh: '不再为别人的眼光而活', titleEn: 'No longer living for others’ opinions',
      bodyZh: '“惧怕人的，陷入网罗；惟有倚靠耶和华的，必得安稳”（29:25）。怕人是很隐蔽的捆绑：为了不被议论，我们答应了不该答应的事；为了讨好，我们隐藏了真实的想法；为了怕失去认可，我们迟迟不做对的选择。解药不是变得更强硬、更不在乎，而是把“最在意谁的看法”这件事，从人转向神。当神的目光成为我的定盘星，别人的眼光就不再能操控我。',
      bodyEn: 'The fear of man proves to be a snare, but whoever puts his trust in the LORD is kept safe (29:25). Fear of people is a hidden bondage: to avoid being talked about, we agree to what we shouldn’t; to please, we hide what we really think; afraid of losing approval, we delay the right choice. The remedy isn’t becoming tougher or more indifferent, but moving the question of “whose opinion matters most” from people to God. When his gaze becomes my compass, others’ opinions can no longer control me.' },
    { tag: 'HUMILITY & PRIDE', tagZh: '谦卑与骄傲', verseRef: 'Proverbs 29:23',
      titleZh: '向下的路，才是向上的路', titleEn: 'The way down is the way up',
      bodyZh: '“人的高傲必使他卑下；心里谦逊的，必得尊荣”（29:23）。这是神国里反常识的规律。骄傲总想抬高自己、抓住话语权、不肯认错，结果反被拖低；谦卑肯承认自己有限、肯听、肯让，反而被神抬举。今天不妨试一次“向下”的选择：主动认一个错、把功劳归给别人、或安静地服事一个不会有人看见的需要。你会发现，那里有出乎意料的自由与尊荣。',
      bodyEn: 'A man’s pride brings him low, but one of lowly spirit gains honor (29:23). This is the upside-down logic of God’s kingdom. Pride always wants to lift itself, seize the last word, and never admit fault — and gets dragged down instead; humility that admits its limits, listens, and yields is the one God raises up. Try one “downward” choice today: own a mistake, give credit to someone else, or quietly serve a need no one will see. You’ll find an unexpected freedom and honor there.' },
  ],

